\date{2026-07-17T15:47:06Z}
\import{base-macros}
\title{Software pipelining vs. loop unrolling}
\tag{public}

\p{See \citet{H.3}{hennessy2019computer} for more details.}

\p{Loop unrolling at its core performs no instruction re-ordering. It literally just expands the loop. When combined with a compiler, this increase the size of your basic blocks allowing for more optimization opportunities. This also eliminates overhead of branch prediction}

\p{[[AOWY]] does not rely on the unroll window.}
\example{
  Loop unrolling cannot make use of the large distance between dependencies for large #{k} below. 
  \codeblock{c}{\verb<<<|
for (i = k; i < n; i++)
    a[i] += a[i - k]; 
  <<<}
  For #{k = 3}, Software pipelining can perform something like
  \codeblock{c}{\verb<<<|
for (i = 3; i < n; i++)
    a[i - 2] += a[i - 2 - k];
    a[i - 1] += a[i - 1 - k];
    a[i] += a[i - k];
  <<<}
  You can imagine how this becomes more useful as #{k} grows large.
}
\example{
  There is an actual dependency between instructions here that cannot be circumvented by [OoO hardware](U0NY) 
\codeblock{c}{\verb<<<|
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i];
<<<}
However, this can be re-written at the software level to get some parallelism within loop bodies as:
\codeblock{c}{\verb<<<|
for (i = 0; i < n; i += 4) {
    s0 += a[i];   s1 += a[i+1];
    s2 += a[i+2]; s3 += a[i+3];
}
sum = (s0 + s1) + (s2 + s3);}
<<<}
}
